# Schema: Polkadot SDK PRDoc Schema (prdoc) v1.0.0
# See doc at https://raw.githubusercontent.com/paritytech/polkadot-sdk/master/prdoc/schema_user.json

title: Collation protocol v4 with bounded segment advertisements

doc:
  - audience: Node Dev
    description: |
      Introduces collation protocol v4. Collators advertise bounded segments of
      chained candidates with a single `AdvertiseSegment` message per scheduling
      parent and core; v4 has no `Declare` and no `CollationSeconded` message. An
      experimental validator side implements segment handling, fetching candidates
      referenced by segment advertisements. The collator side stores immutable
      segments per scheduling parent and core and serves fetches from them. These
      fetches use a new `/req_collation/3` request-response protocol: requests
      identify a candidate by scheduling parent, para id and output head data hash
      instead of relay parent and candidate hash, and responses always include the
      candidate's parent head data. The
      collation generation subsystem accepts multi-candidate segments via
      `SubmitSegment` and distributes them with
      `CollatorProtocolMessage::DistributeSegment`.

      Collation peer-set connections upgrade to the newest common protocol version;
      peers running older nodes keep being served over v3.

      The collation seconded signal is removed end to end: `CollationResult` and
      `SegmentCollation` lose their `result_sender` field, `CollationSecondedSignal`
      is deleted, `WaitToAnnounce` and `ServiceInterface::announce_with_barrier` are
      removed and `CollatorService::new` no longer takes a spawner. The `basic` aura
      collator announces blocks immediately after building, like the lookahead
      collator, instead of waiting for a validator to second the collation.
      Incoming v2/v3 `CollationSeconded` messages are ignored: validators running
      older versions send them unsolicited and the collator no longer tracks
      seconding.
      `RequireSecondedInBlockAnnounce` and `BlockAnnounceData` decoding stay so
      block announcements from nodes running older versions still validate.

      Chains configured with `CollatorSybilResistance::Unresistant` use
      `RequireSecondedInBlockAnnounce`, which bounds block-announce spam by refusing
      announcements at or above the parachain tip unless they carry a seconded
      statement. Removing the seconded signal means updated collators can no longer
      produce that proof, so their tip announcements go out empty and are accepted
      only once the block is backed or included, with `pov-recovery` as the
      propagation backstop. The spam bound itself is unchanged (it now keys on
      backing rather than seconding); what changes is that tip gossip waits for
      backing. Chains using a sybil-resistant collation mechanism such as Aura are
      unaffected: they use `AssumeSybilResistance`, which accepts empty
      announcements outright, because only the slot authority can author.

  - audience: Node Operator
    description: |
      Operators of parachains configured with `CollatorSybilResistance::Unresistant`
      (free-for-all collator sets, without a sybil-resistant authorship mechanism)
      should expect parachain block announcements to propagate slightly later.
      Updated collators announce blocks without a seconded statement attached, and
      such announcements are only accepted at the parachain tip once the block is
      backed or included on the relay chain; `pov-recovery` covers the gap. Chains
      using Aura or another sybil-resistant collation mechanism are unaffected.

      No action is required. Nodes running older versions continue to attach
      seconded statements, and those announcements are still validated, so mixed
      version networks keep working.

